    "q-45649": {
      "dom_id": "step-q45649",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "An economic study in Chile showed that its gross domestic product (GDP) increases fourfold each decade. If the GDP was $$0.5$ billion in $2010$, how many decades will it take for the GDP to increase to $$512$ billion?",
      "worked_solution": "We know that the GDP increases fourfold each decade. So the GDP, which we'll call $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $4.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=0.5$ billion dollars was the GDP in $2010.$\n\n- $b=4$ is the growth factor.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in decades, since $2010.$\n\nTherefore, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=0.5\\cdot {4}^{t}.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the time it takes for the GDP to increase to $$512$ billion, we need to solve the equation $y(t)=512$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =512 \\\\ 0.5\\cdot {4}^{t} & =512 \\\\ {4}^{t} & =\\frac{512}{0.5} \\\\ {4}^{t} & =1\\,024 \\\\ {4}^{t} & ={4}^{5} \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, it will take $5$ decades for the GDP to increase to $$512$ billion.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
