    "q-237332": {
      "dom_id": "step-q237332",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Paul is conducting a scientific experiment with amoebas. He notices that the number of amoebas grows by  $65%$ each day. If there were initially $140$ amoebas, complete the following equation that we should solve to determine how long it will take for the number of amoebas to increase to $800.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,(${{field-2}}$)^{t}=800$",
      "worked_solution": "The amoeba population $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $65%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=140$ is the initial number of amoebas.\n\n- $r=0.65$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in days, since the beginning of the observation.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =140\\cdot (1+0.65)^{t} \\\\  & =140\\cdot (1.65)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the time it will take for the amoeba population to increase to $800,$ we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =800 \\\\ 140\\cdot (1.65)^{t} & =800.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
