    "q-112315": {
      "dom_id": "step-q112315",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "In a certain country, the number of people $P(t)$ who know a second language, $t$ years after $2005,$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\cdot {3}^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ was the number of people who knew a second language in $2005.$ If in $2010$ the number of people who knew a second language was $4\\,860\\,000,$ how many people knew a second language in $2005?$",
      "worked_solution": "The number of people who knew a second language in $2010$ was $4\\,860\\,000,$ this is $5$ years after $2005.$ Thus, $P(5)=4\\,860\\,000.$\n\nTo find ${P}_{0},$ the number of people who knew a second lenguage in $2005,$  we substitute $t=5$ and $P(5)=4\\,860\\,000$ into the given equation and solve for ${P}_{0},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5) & ={P}_{0}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ 4\\,860\\,000 & ={P}_{0}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{4\\,860\\,000}{243} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =20\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in $2005$ there were $20\\,000$ people who knew a second language in that country.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
